hi I'm Barbara Liskov I've been a  professor at MIT since 1972 and I've  done work across a wide range within  computer science including probing  methodology software systems programming  languages and so on  I think we live in the information  age I don't think that those of us  working in computing in the 70s and 80s  really forced saw what was going to  happen how everybody would have access  to computers and the accessing things  over the net the other thing that's  happened recently is the deep learning  that's going on in artificial  intelligence which is allowing all sorts  of things like facial recognition speech  understanding etc and of course this  brings many benefits to people it also  brings across many problems like fake  news and the spread of false information  security problems and so on  I never thought about breaking barriers  I was just very interested in what I was  doing and I just kept going it's funny  though you should ask that question  because when I got my first job as a  programmer there were quite a number of  women doing programming because there  was no computer science education and so  people were being hired from many  different fields if it seemed like they  could do the work so it was better than  probably in terms of proportions not  necessarily better in terms of women  being paid as much as men and so forth  but definitely there were women around  I think we need to consider ethics in  computing and think about what we can do  with software to help solve some of the  problems we have like the and I think  artificial intelligence can help here  you know like the spread of fake fake  news I think that's something we could  tackle I think we could be changing some  of the algorithms that are used to  select what happens but I just think in  general we need to move more ethics into  our community and into our curriculum  and we need to have that be a  consideration and the companies when  they think about what they're doing they  should be thinking about the  ramifications of what they're doing and  how they can make things better
 

